WEBVTT

00:31.860 --> 00:37.540
<i>Very soon, I will finish my life happy.</i>

00:48.340 --> 00:51.370
So, I'm still alive.

01:07.040 --> 01:07.960
Huh?

01:14.590 --> 01:16.250
<i>Schedule notes.</i>

01:16.250 --> 01:20.760
<i>On today's page, 1 entry has been input.</i>

01:20.760 --> 01:25.910
<i>10 AM. August 17th. 
Showa-dori Intersection.</i>

01:28.990 --> 01:31.090
An entry you don't recall?

01:31.090 --> 01:31.990
Yeah.

01:32.800 --> 01:37.760
I have absolutely no memory of it, 
but it was entered into my IP device.

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Fri.</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Sat.</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Sun.</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>10 AM   Showa-dori Intersection</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Mon.</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Tues.</b>

01:42.080 --> 01:54.460
<b>Wed.</b>

01:43.220 --> 01:46.020
I see. How strange.

01:47.070 --> 01:49.190
That's in about two and a half hours.

01:50.220 --> 01:54.460
Some Grandpa from a parallel world 
probably came and input it.

01:55.610 --> 01:59.020
That might be it, actually.

01:59.020 --> 02:05.320
That, or you've gone so senile that you forgot 
you put it in there yourself, Grandpa.

02:05.320 --> 02:06.500
Ai!

02:07.350 --> 02:09.840
I'd say that's the far 
more likely possibility.

02:11.450 --> 02:13.410
So? Are you going?

02:14.620 --> 02:16.230
That's a good question.

02:16.980 --> 02:19.440
It does read like a promise, or something.

02:19.440 --> 02:21.780
I don't think you should go, Grandpa.

02:23.250 --> 02:24.350
Why?

02:24.830 --> 02:27.870
'Cause you'll only realize 
you really have gone senile.

02:27.870 --> 02:30.300
Ai, that's quite enough.

02:30.830 --> 02:33.940
And the chilled chocolate 
is a treat you get at 3 PM.

02:36.820 --> 02:39.890
I thought you were studying 
in your room until lunch?

02:40.430 --> 02:44.160
Which is why I need some 
barley tea as a study buddy.

02:52.730 --> 02:58.550
So, kids these days already know the term 
"parallel world" in elementary school.

02:58.550 --> 03:00.700
Supposedly, they learn it 
in the early grades.

03:00.700 --> 03:02.200
Eri-san mentioned it.

03:03.550 --> 03:04.660
Is that so...

03:05.590 --> 03:07.410
Why not go?

03:08.450 --> 03:10.190
Aren't you curious?

03:11.140 --> 03:13.670
If you go there, you might 
remember something.

03:14.540 --> 03:17.420
That's true. And it's in the neighborhood.

03:24.260 --> 03:25.700
I'll be back in a bit.

03:25.700 --> 03:27.820
Yes, dear. Be careful.

03:30.080 --> 03:31.100
Hold on.

03:32.410 --> 03:34.880
Your hat. I'll go get it.

03:35.750 --> 03:37.690
Oh, that one's fine.

03:38.910 --> 03:40.190
This one?

03:58.460 --> 04:05.470
<b>Please wait until the traffic signal changes.</b>

04:12.530 --> 04:15.430
Tell me, dear. Why are you standing there?

04:15.430 --> 04:16.540
It's dangerous.

04:20.280 --> 04:22.040
You came for me?

04:23.940 --> 04:26.620
Yeah. I'm here.

04:51.440 --> 04:56.400
<i>The light is changing. 
Please exit the crosswalk.</i>

05:02.100 --> 05:05.460
<i>Take care not to stop 
in the middle of crossing.</i>

05:08.660 --> 05:11.660
<b>Please wait until the traffic signal changes.</b>

05:08.660 --> 05:11.660
<b>Please wait until the traffic signal changes.</b>

05:12.420 --> 05:14.590
Did I Parallel Shift?

05:15.380 --> 05:17.120
Display IEPP.

05:23.090 --> 05:24.130
An error?

05:25.100 --> 05:27.000
Where am I?

05:29.390 --> 05:36.330
To Every You I've Loved Before

05:29.390 --> 05:36.330
To Every You I've Loved Before

05:39.070 --> 05:43.310
<i>From the moment they're born, 
people make countless choices.</i>

05:44.350 --> 05:49.050
<i>The worlds with the option you 
didn't choose are alternate realities.</i>

05:49.590 --> 05:52.350
<i>In other words, parallel worlds.</i>

05:52.350 --> 05:54.260
<i>And their existence has now 
been demonstrated.</i>

05:56.900 --> 06:00.830
<i>Moving into a parallel world 
is called Parallel Shifting.</i>

06:01.430 --> 06:05.490
<i>That said, it's not your physical 
body that does the moving.</i>

06:05.490 --> 06:08.440
<i>Your consciousness, termed 
your imaginality, trades places.</i>

06:09.750 --> 06:13.760
<i>Small Parallel Shifts are 
an everyday occurrence,</i>

06:13.760 --> 06:15.850
<i>but you basically never notice.</i>

06:17.090 --> 06:20.160
<i>When you can't find something
 where you left it,</i>

06:20.160 --> 06:23.230
<i>but then it's there when you double-check,</i>

06:23.230 --> 06:26.120
<i>it's often because 
of a small Parallel Shift.</i>

06:28.490 --> 06:30.730
<i>If we say your original world is "0",</i>

06:30.730 --> 06:35.410
<i>you Shifted from world 0, where you left
 the item there, to world 1, where you didn't,</i>

06:35.410 --> 06:37.270
<i>then Shifted back to 0.</i>

06:41.120 --> 06:44.750
<i>The farther from 0 you go, the greater 
the difference in the tangible world.</i>

06:46.020 --> 06:50.600
<i>You might have a different occupation tha
n you did in world 0, or a different spouse.</i>

06:51.670 --> 06:55.190
<i>Still, they're all worlds 
that your choices can lead to.</i>

06:56.890 --> 07:01.380
<i>The first time I made 
a major choice was when I was 7.</i>

07:03.280 --> 07:04.660
Between Dad...

07:04.660 --> 07:06.390
Or Mom...

07:06.390 --> 07:07.900
<i>Who do you wanna go with?</i>

07:08.410 --> 07:12.160
<i>I no longer remember where 
or how exactly they said it.</i>

07:12.700 --> 07:14.910
<i>But that was essentially the situation.</i>

07:15.900 --> 07:18.910
<i>I wonder how I felt, and what I said.</i>

07:20.580 --> 07:22.120
<i>Even after my parents' divorce,</i>

07:22.120 --> 07:25.920
<i>several times a month we'd 
all go on outings together.</i>

07:26.690 --> 07:29.670
<i>But, once fun time was over,</i>

07:29.670 --> 07:34.430
<i>we'd leave my dad, and I'd go with 
my mom back to her childhood home.</i>

07:35.690 --> 07:38.150
<i>Her home was large but very old.</i>

07:38.150 --> 07:41.910
<i>"It was already ancient when 
your old Grandpa was a kid."</i>

07:41.910 --> 07:43.840
<i>Old enough for my grandpa to say that.</i>

07:45.140 --> 07:48.420
<i>Despite there being no second 
floor, it had two entryways,</i>

07:48.420 --> 07:50.320
<i>but it wasn't a two-family home.</i>

07:50.320 --> 07:55.010
<i>The one in front is only ever opened 
during New Year and on special days.</i>

07:55.010 --> 07:57.870
<i>Normally, we just use 
the smaller one in the back.</i>

07:59.100 --> 08:03.150
<i>We lived there with my grandfather, 
my grandmother, and a big dog.</i>

08:05.440 --> 08:08.370
<i>The pond in the inner yard 
didn't have fish or turtles,</i>

08:08.370 --> 08:10.460
<i>and most of the rooms were empty.</i>

08:11.230 --> 08:14.130
<i>The only things it had in excess 
were empty space and ceilings.</i>

08:31.930 --> 08:34.480
Koyomi, where'd you get that?

08:34.480 --> 08:37.820
Dad bought it for me last time I saw him.

08:37.820 --> 08:40.080
It's my present for my eighth birthday!

08:50.760 --> 08:51.900
Yuno.

08:53.220 --> 08:54.320
Bang.

08:57.270 --> 08:59.150
You're too young to have this.

09:00.020 --> 09:03.270
It says for ages 10 and up.

09:03.680 --> 09:04.990
Yeah...

09:04.990 --> 09:07.730
But the other kids have them, too.

09:07.730 --> 09:10.790
You don't need to copy bad stuff 
from the other kids.

09:10.790 --> 09:12.280
Grandpa!

09:22.350 --> 09:26.130
Koyomi, is something wrong?

09:29.780 --> 09:33.550
I'm never talking to Grandpa again!

10:08.740 --> 10:10.080
Mom.

10:10.970 --> 10:12.130
Yes, dear?

10:12.130 --> 10:15.360
Can I take Yuno for her walk?

10:15.360 --> 10:19.590
Go ahead. But come back early so 
you can get ready for the wake.

10:33.950 --> 10:34.950
Yuno?

10:38.020 --> 10:39.320
Geez...

10:40.310 --> 10:42.310
You can't just take off.

10:42.310 --> 10:43.880
Hey! Yuno!

10:43.880 --> 10:44.870
Stop, Yuno!

10:44.870 --> 10:46.830
Wait! W—

11:15.050 --> 11:17.570
Hey. Can you open this?

11:17.570 --> 11:19.120
Open this lid.

11:28.300 --> 11:29.370
Thanks.

11:33.420 --> 11:35.350
Hey, wait a second!

12:29.720 --> 12:32.930
<b>Imaginary Science Research Institute</b>

12:33.820 --> 12:35.700
Imaginary Science...

12:35.700 --> 12:37.740
This is where Dad works.

12:38.420 --> 12:41.480
U-Um! Um, excuse me, ma'am?

12:42.230 --> 12:45.480
Excuse me. Could I borrow your phone?

12:46.780 --> 12:50.950
I was really surprised to get a call 
from you all of a sudden.

12:50.950 --> 12:54.470
You could have just called your dad 
since you were at the Institute.

12:54.470 --> 12:58.160
I don't have Dad's cell phone number, though.

12:58.160 --> 13:00.210
You had mine?

13:00.210 --> 13:04.010
I don't have it. I know it. 
I call it all the time.

13:04.710 --> 13:07.970
What are you talking about? 
You're a goofball.

13:13.120 --> 13:15.250
Koyomi, what are you doing?

13:16.090 --> 13:18.410
Um, nothing. I'm coming.

13:24.870 --> 13:27.830
I'm home. Koyomi's visiting.

13:27.830 --> 13:28.900
Visiting?

13:30.580 --> 13:32.240
Hey there, Koyomi.

13:32.240 --> 13:34.620
Is Mom in the kitchen?

13:34.620 --> 13:35.750
She is.

13:36.650 --> 13:40.750
It's been a while. Tell me, how've you been?

13:42.350 --> 13:45.740
<i>I'm in a different world.</i>

13:44.440 --> 13:47.760
If I'd known I could've 
made something special!

13:47.760 --> 13:53.770
Not that you ever need to call us first. 
This is your home, too, Koyomi.

13:48.330 --> 13:53.130
<i>This is a world where 
I chose to go with my dad.</i>

13:53.130 --> 13:56.610
<i>And it's a world where 
Grandpa's still alive.</i>

13:54.410 --> 13:55.350
Right?

13:55.350 --> 13:56.610
Right.

13:56.610 --> 13:59.340
That's right. Come by any time.

14:00.210 --> 14:04.190
Now then, I think 
I'll go give Yuno her supper.

14:06.670 --> 14:08.280
I'll come with you.

14:10.330 --> 14:11.830
You will?

14:11.830 --> 14:13.540
Let's both go, then.

14:19.170 --> 14:22.540
Yuno, Koyomi came to see us today.

14:31.890 --> 14:33.330
Grandpa.

14:33.330 --> 14:34.560
Ah, Koyomi.

14:34.560 --> 14:35.960
What is it?

14:36.880 --> 14:40.550
Can I sleep in your room with you tonight?

14:40.550 --> 14:42.560
Yes, of course you can.

14:44.950 --> 14:46.300
That reminds me.

14:46.300 --> 14:49.940
Is this the first time you've 
visited since you turned eight?

14:58.120 --> 15:00.070
So Yuno's gone.

15:00.900 --> 15:02.010
Yeah.

15:05.130 --> 15:08.090
There's no way we could 
make it so she's alive again?

15:08.730 --> 15:14.380
Unfortunately, that's not possible. 
The dead can't come back to life.

15:19.040 --> 15:21.710
Grandpa, I'm sorry.

15:21.710 --> 15:24.890
I never got to say sorry after our fight.

15:26.550 --> 15:27.940
Our fight?

15:27.940 --> 15:31.830
Did we have a fight 
the last time you came over?

15:35.080 --> 15:37.710
Did your old Grandpa 
scold you for something?

15:40.540 --> 15:44.020
Even when that happens, it doesn't 
mean Grandpa doesn't love you.

15:44.020 --> 15:45.630
Don't worry.

15:46.070 --> 15:48.560
Really? How come?

15:50.280 --> 15:51.810
How do I explain it?

15:52.530 --> 15:57.890
Would you rather be sad yourself, 
or for someone else to be sad?

15:58.550 --> 16:02.530
Well, I mean... <i>I</i> don't wanna be sad.

16:03.170 --> 16:05.680
Right. And everyone's like that.

16:06.630 --> 16:10.490
So, because of a momentary mood
 or whatever other reasons,

16:10.490 --> 16:13.600
people do things that make others sad.

16:14.970 --> 16:17.560
Usually, they only realize 
their mistake later,

16:17.560 --> 16:21.420
and wonder why they ever did those things.

16:23.960 --> 16:27.340
But at that point, they can't fix it,

16:27.340 --> 16:30.650
so they suffer the pain of that
 sadness over and over.

16:31.630 --> 16:34.550
It's truly a difficult thing to go through.

16:37.740 --> 16:40.110
So you see, your old Grandpa

16:40.110 --> 16:44.870
is only trying to help you avoid that 
based on his own experiences.

16:44.870 --> 16:46.760
Because Grandpa loves you.

16:47.460 --> 16:49.320
Because you love me?

16:49.320 --> 16:51.240
Yup, it sure is.

16:52.720 --> 16:57.000
But y'know, your old Grandpa 
makes mistakes sometimes, too.

16:57.710 --> 17:01.500
So in the end, what you do is
 for you to decide, Koyomi.

17:02.310 --> 17:05.460
You're going to experience 
all sorts of things.

17:06.030 --> 17:11.000
You might encounter situations where 
nobody knows what might happen.

17:11.630 --> 17:15.180
That's just how plentiful 
the choices you have are.

17:16.150 --> 17:17.680
Choices...

17:18.940 --> 17:23.290
<i>Then my grandfather explained 
the significance of possibilities to me,</i>

17:23.290 --> 17:26.730
<i>but at that age, I couldn't 
really wrap my head around it.</i>

17:28.930 --> 17:32.980
It's even hard for your Grandpa 
to figure out a simple explanation.

17:33.860 --> 17:35.660
You're bright, Koyomi,

17:35.660 --> 17:38.740
so once you get it, make sure 
you can explain it clearly to others.

17:39.500 --> 17:40.500
Okay.

17:41.430 --> 17:45.760
Even when you mess up, your 
old Grandpa will never hate you.

17:45.760 --> 17:49.010
So even when Grandpa occasionally 
makes a mistake of his own,

17:49.010 --> 17:51.510
don't go hating him immediately, okay?

17:54.810 --> 17:57.350
I love you, too, Grandpa.

18:18.670 --> 18:20.370
Good morning.

18:22.950 --> 18:24.360
Mom?

18:28.370 --> 18:29.620
What is it?

18:30.370 --> 18:31.910
Where's Grandpa?

18:32.790 --> 18:35.340
The funeral people made 
him look really nice,

18:35.340 --> 18:37.760
and now he's resting in the main hall.

18:42.970 --> 18:44.310
Is something wrong?

18:44.940 --> 18:47.820
Mom, yesterday I...

18:47.820 --> 18:49.970
There's no reason to feel embarrassed.

18:49.970 --> 18:51.880
This is a tough time.

18:51.880 --> 18:55.050
It's perfectly normal to want some company.

18:56.750 --> 18:57.980
Okay.

18:57.980 --> 19:00.580
Now, let's get ready.

19:00.580 --> 19:04.350
We've gotta give Grandpa a proper sendoff.

19:08.730 --> 19:13.550
<i>The man in my grandfather's altar
 photo wore a stern expression,</i>

19:13.550 --> 19:17.420
<i>and looked nothing like the same, 
mellow man from the previous night.</i>

19:18.140 --> 19:21.930
<i>The thin-looking man in the coffin 
didn't look like the picture, either.</i>

19:27.470 --> 19:29.570
I brought fresh water.

19:29.570 --> 19:30.560
Thanks.

19:30.560 --> 19:32.070
Put it down there.

19:33.410 --> 19:37.680
Now, take a look in the top 
section of that tall cabinet.

19:37.680 --> 19:38.690
Okay.

19:38.690 --> 19:40.450
Be careful, honey.

19:40.450 --> 19:41.680
Yes, Grandma.

19:45.970 --> 19:47.790
Mom, look.

19:47.790 --> 19:51.490
Oh my. I'm amazed he saved this.

19:51.490 --> 19:52.330
What do we do with it?

19:52.330 --> 19:55.460
Well, it's not like we need to 
completely empty this room out,

19:55.460 --> 19:56.960
so let's put it back and...

19:57.170 --> 20:01.970
<b></b>Happy 10th birthday, Koyomi.
 Don't point this at people or animals. 

-Grandpa

20:01.970 --> 20:03.720
<b></b>-Grandpa

20:02.810 --> 20:06.820
<i>In that moment, the three versions 
of my grandfather finally merged together,</i>

20:06.820 --> 20:08.720
<i>becoming a single person in my mind.</i>

20:13.020 --> 20:17.360
<i><i>This</i> was the first incident where 
I became conscious of parallel worlds.</i>

20:18.650 --> 20:22.800
<i>The Yuno in my world died 
of old age 2 years later</i>

20:22.800 --> 20:25.950
<i>and now rests in the same place 
I saw in that other world.</i>

20:26.740 --> 20:37.750
<b>Imaginary Science Research Institute</b>

20:28.070 --> 20:30.660
<i>"We have demonstrated 
the existence of parallel worlds."</i>

20:30.660 --> 20:34.750
<i>It was Japan's Imaginary Science Research 
Institute that first announced that.</i>

20:34.750 --> 20:37.750
<i>As in, the research facility 
where my father works.</i>

20:38.780 --> 20:43.200
<i>The research done by him and his 
colleagues garnered global attention.</i>

20:43.200 --> 20:45.260
<i>Perhaps thanks to having a brilliant father,</i>

20:45.760 --> 20:49.740
<i>my grades were abnormally good, 
even if I never did much studying.</i>

20:51.510 --> 20:54.670
<i>Still, having abnormally good grades</i>

20:54.670 --> 20:57.860
<i>basically translates 
to isolation in adolescence.</i>

20:59.620 --> 21:03.220
<i>At least, that's how I thought at the time.</i>

21:04.280 --> 21:07.900
<i>Koyomi, could it be you 
don't have very many friends?</i>

21:07.900 --> 21:11.290
<i>That's how my mother put it. 
But that was incorrect.</i>

21:11.940 --> 21:15.290
<i>In middle school, I didn't 
have a single friend.</i>

21:16.250 --> 21:17.790
<i>So I swore to myself</i>

21:17.790 --> 21:20.300
<i>that in high school I would make friends.</i>

21:21.100 --> 21:25.060
<i>And so, when I was asked to give 
the entrance ceremony speech</i>

21:25.060 --> 21:27.200
<i>due to having the highest 
entrance exam scores,</i>

21:27.200 --> 21:29.080
<i>I politely declined.</i>

21:32.230 --> 21:34.690
<i>That way, I wouldn't draw 
attention to myself.</i>

21:36.140 --> 21:39.470
<i>I wholeheartedly planned to make friends.</i>

21:40.750 --> 21:44.380
<i>But reality was not so obliging.</i>

21:52.330 --> 21:56.240
<b>Prior Academic Year University Entrance Ex
 National & Public University Pas</b>

21:52.330 --> 21:56.240
<b>University of Tokyo
 Tokyo University of Foreign Studies
 Tokyo Institute of Technology
 Hitotsubashi University
 Ochanomizu University
 Tohoku University
 Nagoya University</b>

21:52.840 --> 21:57.430
<i>At that top class feeder school, 
the atmosphere was not at all friendly,</i>

21:58.460 --> 22:03.390
<i>and my high school life hurtled 
yet again toward lonely choices.</i>

22:10.350 --> 22:11.400
Koyomi.

22:15.520 --> 22:18.020
What're you ignoring me for, Koyomi?

22:19.610 --> 22:24.540
Huh? Uh, um... Takigawa-san?

22:25.070 --> 22:28.040
Um, by "Koyomi," do you mean me?

22:28.040 --> 22:29.640
Who else is there?

22:31.110 --> 22:33.030
I'm not angry anymore.

22:34.620 --> 22:35.540
Huh?

22:35.950 --> 22:39.460
If you still want to apologize, 
I'll hear it on the way home.

22:39.460 --> 22:40.380
Oh—

22:40.380 --> 22:43.500
Um, h-hold on a moment, Takigawa-san!

22:45.000 --> 22:47.550
What was with you just now? 
Why are you calling me that?

22:47.550 --> 22:48.690
Huh?

22:48.690 --> 22:50.790
I hate that way of doing things.

22:51.360 --> 22:54.760
Um... is something wrong, Takigawa-san?

22:54.760 --> 22:56.520
Something's definitely off.

22:56.520 --> 22:59.490
It's true that it's felt like 
we're on different pages lately,

22:59.490 --> 23:02.590
but for you to act like this, 
it's not like you, Koyo—

23:10.300 --> 23:11.600
My apologies.

23:31.490 --> 23:33.810
Excuse me, got a sec?

23:35.120 --> 23:36.950
Was there something else?

23:36.950 --> 23:39.350
I need to talk to you. It's for real.

23:40.400 --> 23:42.630
I'll explain everything, so come with me.

23:45.120 --> 23:46.360
Huh?

23:58.610 --> 23:59.760
Karaoke?

24:08.220 --> 24:09.460
You can sit down.

24:12.980 --> 24:14.140
Okay.

24:21.540 --> 24:23.760
Do you know what this is?

24:23.760 --> 24:24.800
Huh?

24:25.580 --> 24:30.290
Um, it's... not a watch, huh?

24:30.290 --> 24:32.150
Have you heard of an IP device?

24:34.060 --> 24:40.240
More or less. It's a device that displays 
which parallel world you're in, right?

24:41.230 --> 24:44.180
If I remember right, they're 
still in the development stage,

24:44.180 --> 24:49.160
and researchers in imaginary science or 
similar fields are doing the testing.

24:49.570 --> 24:51.780
My dad is one of those researchers.

24:52.260 --> 24:55.940
Oh, my dad also works at the—

24:55.940 --> 24:56.880
I know.

24:57.490 --> 24:59.670
I see...

25:00.090 --> 25:05.200
Good. You have the same level of 
knowledge as the Koyomi in my world.

25:06.270 --> 25:08.220
"My world"?

25:09.030 --> 25:11.830
Um... So then, uh...

25:11.830 --> 25:13.690
the Takigawa-san I'm talking to...

25:20.690 --> 25:25.190
From your perspective, I came from 
the world that is a distance of 85 away.

25:25.190 --> 25:26.200
What?

25:27.900 --> 25:32.370
Basically, I hadn't realized 
I had Parallel Shifted

25:32.370 --> 25:36.310
when I called out to you, Koyomi—
I mean, Takasaki-kun.

25:46.150 --> 25:48.540
You can drink that. Aren't you thirsty?

25:53.900 --> 25:57.440
B-But um, why call out to <i>me</i>?

25:59.310 --> 26:00.610
My boyfriend, you mean?

26:01.700 --> 26:03.280
I see.

26:03.280 --> 26:06.600
So, what's my relationship to this boyfriend?

26:20.210 --> 26:22.270
Me? Your boyfriend?!

26:22.890 --> 26:28.560
B-By "boyfriend," you mean we're 
like, dating and everything?

26:28.560 --> 26:30.170
You're a regular Sherlock.

26:30.170 --> 26:32.530
So then, have we, um...

26:32.530 --> 26:34.780
You could really use some tact.

26:39.440 --> 26:40.780
I'm sorry.

26:40.780 --> 26:41.780
Ugh.

26:41.780 --> 26:43.730
This just throws me off!

26:43.730 --> 26:46.180
You're totally different from my Koyomi!

26:47.200 --> 26:48.300
Sorry.

26:49.170 --> 26:53.500
The Koyomi I know is more 
put-together and dependable...

26:54.790 --> 26:55.540
Dependable?

26:55.540 --> 26:56.800
That's right!

26:56.800 --> 27:01.300
When I was about to be attacked by 
a wild dog, you saved me, didn't you?

27:01.900 --> 27:03.280
A wild dog?

27:03.280 --> 27:04.310
When?

27:06.270 --> 27:07.310
Forget it.

27:10.230 --> 27:11.180
Um...

27:13.840 --> 27:17.320
Just to confirm, you <i>are</i>
 Koyomi Takasaki, aren't you?

27:18.100 --> 27:19.110
Well...

27:19.890 --> 27:23.200
I'm Koyomi Takasaki all the time, 
not just when someone's asking.

27:24.130 --> 27:28.270
But I can't really help it if
 I'm different from your Koyomi.

27:28.270 --> 27:31.250
Since 85 should be quite far away,

27:31.250 --> 27:33.740
shouldn't we be almost 
entirely different people?

27:34.270 --> 27:35.730
I'm not so sure.

27:35.730 --> 27:40.880
Well, I mean, I don't know 
the you here at all,

27:40.880 --> 27:42.420
and the one over there doesn't—

27:42.420 --> 27:44.850
"One over there"? Which one of us is that?

27:44.850 --> 27:47.850
Oh, the Takigawa-san from here, 
who's over there...

27:47.850 --> 27:49.100
This is irritating.

27:49.100 --> 27:50.460
Huh?

27:50.460 --> 27:52.660
All this "here" and "there" stuff.

27:52.660 --> 27:55.610
Can't we just distinguish ourselves 
with first and last names?

27:56.360 --> 27:59.110
The us from this world will be 
Takasaki-kun and Takigawa-san.

27:59.110 --> 28:02.240
The us from my world 
will be Koyomi and Kazune.

28:02.970 --> 28:04.460
Which would mean...

28:04.460 --> 28:10.030
It's, um... okay for <i>me</i> to 
call you Kazune? Wait, is it?

28:10.030 --> 28:12.500
Yeah. What's the issue?

28:13.490 --> 28:17.030
I take it that's what 
Koyomi calls you, right?

28:17.790 --> 28:21.510
For me to address you the same way 
he does, it just feels kinda...

28:21.960 --> 28:23.650
It's just a name to use.

28:23.650 --> 28:26.510
Besides, I am who I am.

28:29.150 --> 28:31.020
Well, if you're okay with it... Kazune.

28:31.020 --> 28:32.520
To get back on track,

28:32.920 --> 28:36.950
an IP device reads an imaginality's 
Imaginary Elements Print

28:36.950 --> 28:38.480
and represents it as a number.

28:38.480 --> 28:39.530
Right.

28:40.050 --> 28:43.420
It replaces the number of splits 
with an expression of distance.

28:44.220 --> 28:47.530
A Parallel Shift distance of 85 
is quite far, isn't it?

28:47.530 --> 28:51.090
But until I spoke to you 
after school, Takasaki-kun,

28:51.090 --> 28:53.580
I didn't notice a Shift had occurred.

28:54.280 --> 28:58.830
That's how similar our worlds are. 
They just don't seem 85 apart.

29:00.010 --> 29:01.800
Which got me thinking...

29:02.820 --> 29:07.370
Among the many worlds derived from 
two worlds that were far apart at first,

29:07.370 --> 29:10.860
maybe a series of splits toward 
the same direction occurred,

29:10.860 --> 29:14.320
and then two coincidentally 
similar worlds were produced.

29:14.810 --> 29:18.470
The paths they took were different, 
but they arrived in the same place...

29:18.940 --> 29:20.820
Sort of like fluctuations?

29:21.170 --> 29:26.460
This presupposes that imaginality behaves 
similarly to physical matter, though.

29:26.970 --> 29:28.820
If so, then what happens next is...

29:29.270 --> 29:30.970
Convergence.

29:30.970 --> 29:33.240
The two worlds would overlap each other...

29:33.860 --> 29:37.540
Wait, the two worlds would've 
traced different histories,

29:37.540 --> 29:39.150
so I guess that wouldn't happen.

29:40.310 --> 29:43.980
Like binary stars, they could influence 
each other and remain equidistant...

29:43.980 --> 29:46.840
and continue in the same 
direction. Like that?

29:47.620 --> 29:52.980
The fundamental model of Imaginary Science
likens parallel worlds to bubbles in water.

29:52.980 --> 29:54.640
If we follow that model,

29:54.640 --> 30:00.230
the two bubbles would circle each other 
as they ascend in the same direction.

30:00.230 --> 30:01.440
Something like that.

30:02.090 --> 30:06.290
But right now, you and Koyomi 
are totally different...

30:06.720 --> 30:08.660
Maybe this is over-thinking things,

30:08.660 --> 30:12.200
but could there be some reason 
why Takigawa-san and I

30:12.200 --> 30:14.410
underwent a distance-85 Shift?

30:16.580 --> 30:19.780
The ability to keep the two 
bubbles from separating...

30:20.940 --> 30:22.380
What do you make of that?

30:22.380 --> 30:24.830
Well, for instance, Takigawa-san—

30:24.830 --> 30:28.380
I mean, Kazune dating me is an option.

30:29.310 --> 30:31.390
As in <i>me</i> dating you, Takasaki-kun?

30:31.390 --> 30:32.670
Oh—

30:32.670 --> 30:33.970
S-Sorry!

30:33.970 --> 30:35.890
My bad. That's too much of a leap.

30:35.890 --> 30:38.370
No, wait, I mean... I got confused...

30:39.450 --> 30:43.030
I don't really like-<i>like </i>you, Takasaki-kun.

30:43.630 --> 30:44.840
Right. Duh.

30:44.840 --> 30:48.040
But, I <i>do</i> adore Koyomi,

30:48.700 --> 30:52.910
so it might be a possibility for Takigawa-san
to like-<i>like</i> you, too, Takasaki-kun.

30:53.400 --> 30:57.240
There should be a path of choices 
for you to reach that point as well.

30:57.950 --> 31:00.920
Well, if you're interested, go for it.

31:04.080 --> 31:08.160
I don't know what will happen, 
but let's keep this a secret for now.

31:08.770 --> 31:10.620
I'd prefer to avoid a fuss.

31:11.340 --> 31:13.100
And let's leave here separately.

31:13.790 --> 31:16.190
Yeah. Good point.

31:16.930 --> 31:18.570
I'll pay.

31:18.570 --> 31:20.790
You're a guest here, Kazune.

31:20.790 --> 31:23.070
An amusing way to look at it.

31:23.070 --> 31:24.160
Thanks.

31:25.560 --> 31:30.070
Once we leave, act naturally. 
Go about life the same as before.

31:30.740 --> 31:33.060
Yeah. Naturally. Got it.

31:33.450 --> 31:36.390
That's right. Go with the flow.

31:37.460 --> 31:41.460
Well, who knows if I'll see you tomorrow.

31:41.460 --> 31:42.630
Huh?

31:42.630 --> 31:44.590
Oh. Yeah.

31:45.960 --> 31:46.870
Goodbye.

31:48.890 --> 31:49.950
Goodbye.

31:51.300 --> 31:52.970
<i>With those final words,</i>

31:52.970 --> 31:58.280
<i>the Takigawa-san I'd never seen before, 
"Kazune" from world 85 and I parted ways.</i>

32:02.750 --> 32:05.420
Parallel world 85...

32:14.920 --> 32:18.880
<i>The next day, she came to 
school like any other day.</i>

32:22.570 --> 32:23.750
<i>But,</i>

32:23.750 --> 32:25.370
<i>was it Kazune?</i>

32:25.370 --> 32:26.850
<i>Or was it Takigawa-san?</i>

32:30.300 --> 32:34.290
<i>With a distance as large as 85, there 
had to be </i>some<i> difference in demeanor.</i>

32:35.230 --> 32:38.760
<i>But the way she carried herself 
only read as "Kazune" to me.</i>

32:39.970 --> 32:44.260
<i>Of course, I'd never even said 
hello to Takigawa-san before,</i>

32:44.260 --> 32:47.470
<i>so I had no point of comparison 
for how different they might be.</i>

32:49.490 --> 32:51.030
<i>And the next day,</i>

32:51.030 --> 32:54.380
<i>and the next day after that, 
and the next next day after that,</i>

32:54.970 --> 32:57.030
<i>I saw her, of course.</i>

32:58.390 --> 33:00.100
<i>Except I didn't speak to her.</i>

33:01.710 --> 33:02.980
<i>I couldn't.</i>

33:07.420 --> 33:11.000
<i>Her world had its own version of me.</i>

33:15.810 --> 33:19.150
<i>Another me, not the one here now.</i>

33:37.830 --> 33:39.550
<i>In that world,</i>

33:39.550 --> 33:42.990
<i>she and I were apparently going out
 as boyfriend and girlfriend.</i>

33:49.950 --> 33:53.210
<i>The other... me and her...</i>

34:08.600 --> 34:11.610
<b>Cigarettes   Liquor</b>

34:12.020 --> 34:13.640
<i>Hey, so...</i>

34:13.640 --> 34:19.040
<i>is it possible to go to, or return to, 
a particular parallel world?</i>

34:19.040 --> 34:21.120
<i>Theoretically, yes, it's possible.</i>

34:21.760 --> 34:25.240
<i>And in actuality we're currently 
researching Optional Shifting,</i>

34:25.240 --> 34:29.870
<i>meaning Shifting to an arbitrary world.</i>

34:29.870 --> 34:31.390
<i>You are? So then—</i>

34:31.390 --> 34:35.010
<i>However, that doesn't mean 
the technology is established.</i>

34:35.010 --> 34:39.130
<i>I expect it'll take... 10 more years 
to reach practical realization.</i>

34:40.090 --> 34:43.140
I see... Thanks.

34:43.690 --> 34:45.760
Me too. Talk to you soon.

34:47.270 --> 34:49.140
10 years, huh?

35:05.770 --> 35:07.160
Morning, Kazune.

35:11.110 --> 35:12.060
Kazune?

35:20.190 --> 35:21.870
<i>That's Takigawa-san.</i>

35:27.080 --> 35:29.860
<i>So Kazune made it home.</i>

35:46.490 --> 35:48.700
<b></b>Takigawa-san

35:57.910 --> 36:00.470
Hey. Sorry for calling you here.

36:02.080 --> 36:03.220
Then this was...

36:03.220 --> 36:04.470
Yeah.

36:04.920 --> 36:07.280
Well, come on in and sit down.

36:14.580 --> 36:16.130
Want something to drink?

36:16.130 --> 36:17.160
No.

36:18.290 --> 36:20.260
Oh, wanna sing something?

36:20.260 --> 36:20.930
Excuse me?

36:20.930 --> 36:22.140
Erm, never mind...

36:28.700 --> 36:32.960
Um, so... I'll just 
get straight to the point.

36:33.920 --> 36:35.720
Like I wrote in the note,

36:35.720 --> 36:39.860
you were in parallel world 85 
for a while recently, right?

36:41.110 --> 36:42.610
Yes.

36:42.610 --> 36:45.020
So then, um...

36:45.020 --> 36:47.130
How was it?

36:47.820 --> 36:49.590
What do you mean?

36:49.590 --> 36:52.160
Erm, well, um...

36:53.230 --> 36:55.060
With the me over there...

37:02.140 --> 37:06.200
No, forget it. Sorry.

37:08.170 --> 37:10.710
How were things here?

37:10.710 --> 37:12.810
Huh? Right.

37:13.230 --> 37:14.590
Yeah.

37:14.590 --> 37:17.030
I had a talk here. With Kazune.

37:17.620 --> 37:18.710
Kazune?

37:18.710 --> 37:20.350
Oh, sorry!

37:20.350 --> 37:21.880
If you don't like it, I'll stop.

37:24.690 --> 37:26.800
No. It doesn't bother me.

37:28.780 --> 37:30.610
Could I see the menu?

37:30.610 --> 37:32.810
Uh, yeah. Sure.

37:34.700 --> 37:35.680
Here.

37:41.000 --> 37:43.070
What was your impression?

37:43.070 --> 37:44.260
Huh?

37:44.260 --> 37:46.820
Of... that "Kazune."

37:48.060 --> 37:52.830
I guess that Kazune was 
in love with Koyomi.

37:54.090 --> 37:55.330
In love?

37:55.330 --> 37:56.080
Yeah.

37:56.970 --> 37:59.210
Oh, but I don't mean with <i>me</i>, of course.

37:59.210 --> 38:03.600
By "Koyomi" I mean the one from that world, 
the Koyomi Takasaki of world 85.

38:04.660 --> 38:06.190
Is that so?

38:06.600 --> 38:08.940
Yeah. It is.

38:12.480 --> 38:14.590
Look, I'm not saying it's 
a good reason, but...

38:16.300 --> 38:20.580
Takigawa-san, w-would...

38:23.410 --> 38:26.540
would you be f-friends with me?

38:32.530 --> 38:34.370
Be... friends?

38:34.370 --> 38:36.180
Uh, yeah.

38:47.440 --> 38:48.630
Takigawa-san!

38:57.140 --> 38:58.380
Takigawa-san?

38:58.790 --> 39:03.300
S-So... you go with... "friends" there?

39:03.300 --> 39:04.330
Huh?

39:05.620 --> 39:07.150
Hey, take a look.

39:13.100 --> 39:14.160
Huh?

39:16.670 --> 39:18.450
Ka-Kazune?!

39:18.450 --> 39:20.920
Y-You tricked me!

39:20.920 --> 39:25.030
And here I was, being happy for you 
for getting to go back to your world!

39:25.030 --> 39:26.700
You've got it all wrong.

39:27.290 --> 39:28.340
The thing is...

39:29.570 --> 39:30.640
See?

39:37.290 --> 39:38.340
A sticker?

39:38.340 --> 39:40.260
Pretty well-made, right?

39:40.260 --> 39:43.480
Um, uh, so this means...

39:43.480 --> 39:49.030
Meaning, I was never off in any
 parallel world from the start.

39:50.320 --> 39:54.150
I've been the Kazune Takigawa 
of this world the whole time.

39:54.910 --> 39:58.330
There never <i>was</i> a Kazune 85.

39:59.240 --> 40:01.060
Wh-Why?

40:01.950 --> 40:03.330
Why do all that?

40:05.730 --> 40:07.420
To get back at you.

40:07.420 --> 40:08.840
To get back at me?

40:09.330 --> 40:11.840
Because you forgot about me.

40:11.840 --> 40:13.340
Huh?

40:17.300 --> 40:20.790
As I was saying, you had 
the top entrance exam scores,

40:20.790 --> 40:23.570
yet you declined the ceremony speech, right?

40:23.570 --> 40:26.940
Uh, yeah... I did.

40:26.940 --> 40:30.730
The student who gave 
the speech instead of you

40:30.730 --> 40:31.760
was me.

40:33.100 --> 40:34.360
Was it?

40:34.700 --> 40:36.370
See? You don't even remember!

40:36.840 --> 40:40.070
I was so proud of myself.

40:40.070 --> 40:42.010
But I didn't actually earn it.

40:42.010 --> 40:43.340
How could I let that go?!

40:47.040 --> 40:52.610
Um, I don't suppose <i>that's</i> the whole 
reason you wanted to get back at me?

40:54.550 --> 40:57.140
It's how a person would react normally.

40:59.660 --> 41:02.150
I don't think they would, normally.

41:04.300 --> 41:07.830
Look, I just wanted to avoid standing out.

41:08.840 --> 41:10.680
I guess it caused a problem...

41:14.340 --> 41:15.660
What's this for?

41:17.590 --> 41:18.830
Reconciliation.

41:19.460 --> 41:22.260
I feel better after laughing that hard.

41:23.180 --> 41:24.660
Thanks.

41:36.910 --> 41:37.820
Kazune.

41:37.820 --> 41:39.090
Yes?

41:39.090 --> 41:42.660
W-Would you go out with me?

41:42.660 --> 41:43.540
No.

41:43.540 --> 41:44.790
Huh?

41:44.790 --> 41:46.190
I told you before.

41:46.190 --> 41:50.190
I like Koyomi. He's a more
 dependable person.

41:54.900 --> 41:56.780
So my answer to Takasaki-kun

41:56.780 --> 41:57.790
is no!

41:58.250 --> 42:01.940
<i>And thus my very first 
confession of feelings...</i>

42:01.940 --> 42:05.080
Keep things like before and 
don't say hi when you see me.

42:06.920 --> 42:08.940
<i>...ended in spectacular failure.</i>

42:11.890 --> 42:14.500
What's even going on here? Man...

42:21.460 --> 42:24.350
<i>Nevertheless, even though 
she said not to even greet her,</i>

42:24.850 --> 42:27.850
<i>it was Kazune who first struck up 
our next conversation.</i>

42:28.380 --> 42:29.380
What is it?

42:30.060 --> 42:31.100
This.

42:31.780 --> 42:34.340
95, eh? You were really close.

42:34.340 --> 42:35.860
Not that.

42:35.860 --> 42:37.570
<b>First-Years First Semester Mid-term Exam Answer Sheet 
Math I</b>

42:36.320 --> 42:37.570
This question.

42:38.350 --> 42:41.050
The teacher said only you 
got it, Takasaki-kun.

42:41.050 --> 42:43.120
Oh? Huh.

42:44.470 --> 42:46.420
Tell me.

42:46.420 --> 42:49.460
Why were you the only one not 
fooled by the tricky question?

42:49.890 --> 42:53.840
It wasn't tricky so much as something 
not covered yet in class.

42:53.840 --> 42:55.220
Really?

42:55.220 --> 42:56.130
Yeah.

42:56.130 --> 42:59.700
Except, if you expand 
the previous concepts...

43:00.350 --> 43:02.670
<i>Perhaps her previous prank 
hadn't been enough,</i>

43:02.670 --> 43:04.850
<i>because she started competing 
with me in everything.</i>

43:05.590 --> 43:08.990
<i>But I don't know whether to call 
her methods forceful, or meek.</i>

43:08.990 --> 43:10.220
Oh.

43:11.100 --> 43:12.800
I see it now.

43:13.510 --> 43:14.580
Thanks.

43:17.130 --> 43:19.250
Whoa, she was kinda cool.

43:19.250 --> 43:20.110
Huh?

43:20.760 --> 43:22.180
Takasaki-kun.

43:22.180 --> 43:25.440
You didn't get a 100 on
 the Classical Japanese test.

43:25.990 --> 43:27.330
Need any advice?

43:28.180 --> 43:30.550
For that, I don't really...

43:30.550 --> 43:31.830
I knew it!

43:31.830 --> 43:33.230
You weren't trying.

43:33.230 --> 43:35.340
You need to take it seriously!

43:36.530 --> 43:37.660
Why?

43:38.270 --> 43:40.340
<i>I don't really get why,</i>

43:40.340 --> 43:42.870
<i>but during lunch or after school</i>

43:42.870 --> 43:45.860
<i>Kazune and I started little 
semi-study sessions.</i>

43:47.340 --> 43:49.560
<i>At the beginning I was distrustful,</i>

43:49.560 --> 43:53.250
<i>but when all is said and done, 
I was happy to get to talk with her,</i>

43:53.250 --> 43:55.610
<i>so I started participating in earnest, too.</i>

43:56.480 --> 44:00.110
<i>Our study sessions became 
a great source of joy for me,</i>

44:00.110 --> 44:04.430
<i>but perhaps it looked like the two top 
students were pushing each other farther,</i>

44:04.430 --> 44:07.330
<i>because our classmates gradually joined us,</i>

44:07.830 --> 44:10.830
<i>and just like that, our 
private bubble was popped.</i>

44:11.510 --> 44:15.060
<i>"Ugh, if you wanna raise your 
grades, go to a cram school."</i>

44:15.060 --> 44:17.320
<i>I had that thought a few times, to be honest.</i>

44:18.790 --> 44:23.950
<i>Still, the study sessions spurred 
the class to talk to each other more,</i>

44:23.950 --> 44:26.690
<i>and in part because 
everyone's grades went up,</i>

44:26.690 --> 44:30.080
<i>the mood in the classroom 
became increasingly congenial.</i>

44:33.050 --> 44:35.500
<i>I even found people I could call friends.</i>

44:36.650 --> 44:38.980
<i>But still, even after we got close...</i>

44:38.980 --> 44:42.660
Well, we all know Takasaki 
lacks a human heart.

44:42.660 --> 44:44.490
They'd occasionally say stuff like <i>that</i>.

44:45.190 --> 44:46.350
<i>And with Kazune...</i>

44:46.350 --> 44:48.740
"You really need a sense of tact."

44:48.740 --> 44:50.310
<i>...was a frequent refrain.</i>

44:51.070 --> 44:54.070
<i>Basically, they said I didn't
 have a human heart,</i>

44:54.070 --> 44:58.500
<i>and therefore I was 
unable to be considerate.</i>

44:58.500 --> 45:02.220
What's more, you have 
absolutely no awareness of it.

45:06.430 --> 45:08.010
<i>That couldn't be true.</i>

45:09.010 --> 45:12.270
<i>During high school, with 
my friends' collaboration</i>

45:12.270 --> 45:15.010
<i>—well, more amusement-motivated help—</i>

45:15.010 --> 45:16.600
<i>I confessed to Kazune.</i>

45:17.340 --> 45:18.270
Kazune.

45:19.170 --> 45:20.240
What is it?

45:21.480 --> 45:24.840
I think you already know, 
but, I really do like you.

45:25.510 --> 45:26.540
Will you—

45:28.990 --> 45:30.030
No.

45:46.250 --> 45:50.090
<i>I don't have a human heart? 
Could they be any more wrong?</i>

45:54.020 --> 45:56.500
<i>Kazune wouldn't engage at all,</i>

45:56.500 --> 45:59.960
<i>and even breezily sidestepped 
my confession on graduation day.</i>

46:02.470 --> 46:05.210
<i>I enrolled at the first 
university in the world</i>

46:05.210 --> 46:08.060
<i>to establish an imaginary 
science department.</i>

46:09.100 --> 46:11.350
And so did Kazune.

46:13.790 --> 46:15.390
By the way,

46:15.390 --> 46:17.650
there's something I want to ask you.

46:17.650 --> 46:19.060
What is it?

46:19.060 --> 46:21.020
Wanna go out?

46:21.640 --> 46:24.240
Sure. Where to?

46:37.530 --> 46:39.770
<i>And so, we started going out.</i>

46:39.770 --> 46:41.940
<b></b>An unexpected error occurred. 
Program halted. 
Error Code (S0108)

46:42.740 --> 46:46.890
<i>Kazune fundamentally maintained 
her silent, cool, brilliant style,</i>

46:46.890 --> 46:51.210
<i>but when we were alone, she'd make 
jokes or be more sweet and vulnerable.</i>

46:51.940 --> 46:55.200
<i>But, she'd often get angry for
 reasons I didn't understand,</i>

46:55.560 --> 46:59.520
<i>and as a result, our relationship 
was often on-again off-again.</i>

46:59.520 --> 47:01.620
Ta-ka-sa-ki!

47:07.070 --> 47:07.960
Morning.

47:08.450 --> 47:09.300
Morning.

47:10.070 --> 47:11.470
You look sleepy.

47:11.470 --> 47:13.470
Yeah. I <i>am</i> sleepy.

47:24.470 --> 47:25.480
Your hair!

47:27.490 --> 47:32.330
<b>Imaginary Science Research Institute</b>

47:27.490 --> 47:32.330
<b>Imaginary Science Research Institute</b>

47:28.260 --> 47:33.130
<i>After graduating, we both joined 
the Imaginary Science Research Institute,</i>

47:33.130 --> 47:35.190
<i>where my father served as Deputy Director.</i>

47:37.660 --> 47:39.070
What's wrong?

47:39.070 --> 47:40.080
Hmm.

47:40.600 --> 47:42.250
You've been here before.

47:42.250 --> 47:43.250
Huh?

47:43.940 --> 47:46.910
Multiple times, between 
the tours and interviews.

47:47.820 --> 47:50.010
Oh, right. That's true.

47:50.720 --> 47:55.010
<i>At this time, IP devices were just 
on the verge of practical realization,</i>

47:55.010 --> 47:57.180
<i>but they were occasionally malfunctioning.</i>

47:58.570 --> 48:04.470
<i>The first team we joined was assigned
 to improve their reliability.</i>

49:18.520 --> 49:19.600
I think this will work.

49:19.600 --> 49:21.040
I think so, too.

49:22.630 --> 49:23.810
This is pretty good.

49:23.810 --> 49:25.660
Actually, it's perfect.

49:26.420 --> 49:28.040
Thanks for letting me use this.

49:28.580 --> 49:30.860
Calligraphy pens are 
pretty easy to use, huh?

49:31.810 --> 49:33.770
It's eyeliner.

49:33.770 --> 49:34.770
Eyeli—

49:38.080 --> 49:41.440
Are you actually gonna 
tell me you never noticed?

49:41.440 --> 49:42.740
Do you have a moment?

49:42.740 --> 49:43.800
Sure!

49:47.990 --> 49:50.410
The input on smart phones is time-consuming,

49:50.410 --> 49:54.340
and the small screen makes it 
hard to see the entire formula.

49:54.340 --> 49:57.120
So, what'd your mom say?

49:57.120 --> 50:00.630
You mean about the dry 
cleaning bill? I'll cover it.

50:01.690 --> 50:02.990
No need.

50:03.740 --> 50:05.670
Oh, I see. Sorry.

50:06.210 --> 50:07.720
I'll pay you back for the shirt.

50:07.720 --> 50:08.750
It's <i>fine</i>.

50:19.290 --> 50:24.200
<i>The idea Kazune and I had drastically 
improved IP device performance.</i>

50:25.830 --> 50:28.290
<i>Due to that, it was discovered</i>

50:28.290 --> 50:32.300
<i>that people routinely Shift to extremely 
close parallel worlds with no awareness.</i>

50:33.880 --> 50:36.850
<i>The devices were even 
distributed to general testers,</i>

50:36.850 --> 50:39.400
<i>and the collection of further data began.</i>

50:41.990 --> 50:44.390
Please do me the honor of marrying me.

50:52.740 --> 50:54.400
You got an aquamarine.

50:54.400 --> 50:58.440
Yeah. I heard it's the birthstone for March.

51:00.040 --> 51:02.280
This is well done for you, Koyomi.

51:04.350 --> 51:07.380
Something wrong? It's the left ring finger.

51:11.220 --> 51:12.220
Thank you.

51:12.220 --> 51:15.420
Why say that? It's the 
beginning, not the end.

51:16.120 --> 51:18.060
To our wonderful future.

51:23.930 --> 51:27.060
I guess it's a little cold now 
for having a beer outside.

51:27.060 --> 51:28.410
Is it?

51:28.410 --> 51:31.870
Hmm, well, I <i>do</i> think it'd 
be nice to have a glass.

51:32.850 --> 51:37.390
Also, there's supposed to be
 a nifty view here at night.

51:38.100 --> 51:40.150
But it looks like I got the time wrong.

51:42.630 --> 51:45.660
Now that's much more like you, Takasaki-kun.

51:45.660 --> 51:47.050
What do you mean by that?

51:47.050 --> 51:50.280
I'll look forward to seeing that view.

51:51.320 --> 51:52.350
Okay?

51:55.790 --> 51:56.840
Is something wrong?

51:57.390 --> 51:58.920
I don't see my bag.

51:59.500 --> 52:00.660
Other side.

52:00.660 --> 52:01.700
What?

52:04.070 --> 52:05.430
That's strange.

52:06.070 --> 52:08.620
I could swear I put it on my right.

52:12.940 --> 52:15.940
Sorry. Where were we?

52:17.410 --> 52:18.800
How far back should I go?

52:19.730 --> 52:21.950
<i>I suddenly had this thought.</i>

52:22.500 --> 52:25.780
<i>Was it "Kazune 0" who accepted the ring?</i>

52:26.340 --> 52:28.990
<i>Was I "0" at the time?</i>

52:30.360 --> 52:31.360
Come in.

52:32.930 --> 52:35.760
Koyomi Takasaki, sir, it's time.

52:51.250 --> 52:53.070
No IP device.

52:53.070 --> 52:55.180
Yeah. You, too.

52:55.180 --> 52:56.220
Yes.

53:25.510 --> 53:28.760
<i>Who exactly will I be marrying?</i>

53:35.310 --> 53:37.860
<i>As research on parallel worlds advanced,</i>

53:38.300 --> 53:41.290
<i>parallel worlds communicated with each other.</i>

53:41.290 --> 53:43.780
<i>Consequently, the world quantum computerized,</i>

53:44.340 --> 53:49.780
<i>and not just imaginary science, but all 
science and tech development accelerated.</i>

53:51.290 --> 53:56.160
<i>At the same time, the front entryway at 
my home opened twice in quick succession,</i>

53:56.790 --> 53:58.940
<i>making things pretty lonely.</i>

54:00.450 --> 54:02.800
<i>But we also welcomed new family.</i>

54:03.320 --> 54:04.450
What color?

54:04.450 --> 54:05.480
Orange!

54:06.150 --> 54:07.450
Look, right here.

54:07.450 --> 54:09.050
Oh, there it is.

54:11.990 --> 54:14.070
What are you drawing?

54:14.070 --> 54:15.260
I dunno.

54:15.950 --> 54:18.530
You don't know what it is?

54:18.530 --> 54:19.520
Nope.

54:20.020 --> 54:22.920
I don't know what it's called, 
but it was cool.

54:22.920 --> 54:24.320
Oh, I see.

54:24.320 --> 54:25.970
Where did you see it?

54:25.970 --> 54:30.870
Um, well... I went to see it 
with Papa one day.

54:30.870 --> 54:32.270
Where?

54:32.270 --> 54:33.650
I don't know.

54:34.420 --> 54:35.560
It's a secret.

54:35.560 --> 54:37.860
Oh? Where is it?

54:37.860 --> 54:39.160
It's a secret!

54:39.820 --> 54:41.650
Hey, tell Mommy!

54:41.650 --> 54:43.190
I'm not telling!

54:44.710 --> 54:47.690
Mama, I'm trying to draw!

54:47.690 --> 54:49.850
Come on, tell me!

54:49.850 --> 54:52.010
Coochie-coochie-coo!

54:52.010 --> 54:55.060
Sorry to interrupt the fun, 
but we'd better get going.

54:55.060 --> 54:59.260
Get this. Ryou won't tell me 
what place he's drawing.

54:59.260 --> 55:00.900
Papa, you'll know!

55:00.900 --> 55:02.610
It's a place you like.

55:02.610 --> 55:05.320
Hmm, I'm not sure.

55:05.320 --> 55:07.170
Well, it's not finished yet.

55:09.360 --> 55:11.500
Okay, time to get going.

55:11.500 --> 55:12.720
Aww.

55:12.720 --> 55:14.950
Can I draw a little more first?

55:14.950 --> 55:16.210
Please!

55:17.150 --> 55:18.330
What if...

55:18.330 --> 55:22.670
just for today, we leave the crayons out?

55:22.670 --> 55:24.310
We aren't picking up?

55:24.310 --> 55:27.730
That way you can go back to 
drawing as soon as you get home.

55:27.730 --> 55:28.740
Okay!

55:31.340 --> 55:33.720
Make sure not to step on them, okay?

55:33.720 --> 55:35.350
I know.

55:35.350 --> 55:37.490
Incredible!

55:45.360 --> 55:49.360
<i>The grounds contain low-illumination 
areas for the purposes of the exhibit.</i>

55:50.400 --> 55:56.390
<i>"Fantastical Zoo" is a work of moving 
monumental sculpture by Claire Udesky.</i>

55:56.390 --> 55:58.160
<i>The names of the individual works are...</i>

56:03.880 --> 56:07.030
The line's not moving. Looks like
 it'll take a little longer.

56:07.670 --> 56:10.380
Maybe five or ten minutes.

56:10.990 --> 56:12.510
Where are you right now?

56:12.510 --> 56:14.470
<i>The hill in the central plaza.</i>

56:14.470 --> 56:16.420
<i>By the fog whatchamacallit.</i>

56:18.230 --> 56:19.320
Kazune?

56:20.370 --> 56:21.390
Hello?

56:27.280 --> 56:28.300
Run!

56:41.880 --> 56:44.000
Ryou? Ryou!

56:48.000 --> 56:49.840
Ryou, I'm over here!

56:51.300 --> 56:52.430
Mama!

57:01.850 --> 57:02.860
Ryou!

57:50.110 --> 57:51.410
What's wrong?

57:53.710 --> 57:56.450
Nothing, I just had a nightmare.

57:57.520 --> 58:00.010
It's no wonder after what happened.

58:00.010 --> 58:00.970
Yeah.

58:00.970 --> 58:03.010
But it's all right.

58:03.010 --> 58:05.640
There's nothing to worry about now.

58:05.640 --> 58:06.880
That's true.

58:07.720 --> 58:10.980
They even apprehended the guy at the scene.

58:15.470 --> 58:18.570
I'm just glad Ryou is okay.

58:19.340 --> 58:22.130
And all thanks to you
 tackling the guy, Koyomi.

58:22.650 --> 58:24.390
I seemed kinda cool, right?

58:25.180 --> 58:28.870
I guess I've managed to be more 
like Kazune's version of me.

58:28.870 --> 58:30.170
Huh?

58:30.170 --> 58:32.980
Remember? What you said way back when.

58:32.980 --> 58:36.660
That the Koyomi you knew 
was a more dependable person.

58:42.130 --> 58:45.590
I'm so glad that nothing 
happened to you and Ryou.

58:47.260 --> 58:48.370
Kazune...

59:12.100 --> 59:14.730
<i>It's not healthy to let this 
keep a grip on us,</i>

59:14.730 --> 59:17.530
<i>so I think we should try 
to get back to normal.</i>

59:18.330 --> 59:21.670
How do you feel about starting 
kindergarten again next week?

59:21.670 --> 59:23.030
I wanna go!

59:23.030 --> 59:24.260
I wanna go to school!

59:24.260 --> 59:25.520
No!

59:25.520 --> 59:28.290
What'll we do if we let him out 
and something happens?!

59:31.450 --> 59:32.910
It's okay.

59:32.910 --> 59:36.300
Mama's not angry. Right?

59:38.210 --> 59:39.720
Did I startle you?

59:39.720 --> 59:40.800
I'm sorry, honey.

59:41.710 --> 59:44.530
Oh, hey. What do you want for lunch?

59:44.530 --> 59:47.810
Huh? Mama, we just ate breakfast.

59:49.390 --> 59:52.210
They're gonna crash!

59:54.330 --> 59:55.320
I'm off to work.

59:55.320 --> 59:57.610
Have a good day, Papa!

59:57.610 --> 59:59.050
Be safe.

59:59.050 --> 1:00:01.120
Anything I should grab on the way home?

1:00:01.880 --> 1:00:03.140
Let's see...

1:00:04.070 --> 1:00:05.780
I think we're good.

1:00:05.780 --> 1:00:09.330
Can I fly the Flying Humanoid 
outside through the window?

1:00:09.330 --> 1:00:11.370
The wind will carry it off and it'll get lost.

1:00:11.370 --> 1:00:12.340
What?

1:00:12.340 --> 1:00:14.340
See, I told you.

1:00:16.550 --> 1:00:20.020
Hey, Ryou. Remember your drawing?

1:00:20.020 --> 1:00:21.570
You don't want to finish it?

1:00:23.250 --> 1:00:26.350
I don't remember what I was gonna draw.

1:00:30.730 --> 1:00:32.350
How's Kazune-kun?

1:00:33.020 --> 1:00:36.610
Immediately after, she was calmer than I was,

1:00:36.610 --> 1:00:38.880
and I thought, "That's so like her."

1:00:40.040 --> 1:00:42.720
But for the past three days, it's like...

1:00:43.680 --> 1:00:46.570
she won't leave Ryou's side 
even for a moment.

1:00:46.570 --> 1:00:49.800
Well, that was quite a thing to go through.

1:00:49.800 --> 1:00:52.180
Isn't that a natural reaction for a mother?

1:00:52.740 --> 1:00:54.460
I suppose so...

1:00:55.110 --> 1:00:56.110
But...

1:00:57.800 --> 1:00:59.910
Don't worry too much.

1:00:59.910 --> 1:01:03.000
You're okay because you protected Ryou,

1:01:03.000 --> 1:01:06.320
but if you hadn't, who knows how you'd react?

1:01:06.320 --> 1:01:07.830
If I hadn't?

1:01:09.260 --> 1:01:12.120
Pardon me. Do you have a moment?

1:01:12.120 --> 1:01:13.100
What is it?

1:01:13.100 --> 1:01:15.960
Deputy Director, I need to speak with you.

1:01:22.380 --> 1:01:26.230
We received an emergency message 
from the Imaginary Science Agency.

1:01:26.230 --> 1:01:27.490
Emergency?

1:01:27.490 --> 1:01:31.120
Regarding the newly established 
Shift Monitoring Office.

1:01:43.090 --> 1:01:47.070
The relative SIP value is 22 plus 10?

1:01:47.070 --> 1:01:49.630
The issue is the Shift distance.

1:01:50.070 --> 1:01:51.790
I'm going to leave early today.

1:01:52.350 --> 1:01:54.080
Stop, Koyomi.

1:01:54.640 --> 1:01:59.650
Apparently an unauthorized Optional Shift 
targeting Kazune-kun has been confirmed.

1:02:00.830 --> 1:02:02.650
<i>An Optional Shift.</i>

1:02:02.650 --> 1:02:07.200
<i>A Shift where a magnetic device is used to 
artificially cause a Parallel Shift</i>

1:02:07.200 --> 1:02:10.240
<i>and swap into a designated parallel world.</i>

1:02:09.410 --> 1:02:11.410
<b></b>Kazune

1:02:10.830 --> 1:02:11.410
<b></b>Calling

1:02:12.370 --> 1:02:15.470
<i>Optional Shifting experiments 
began to be conducted</i>

1:02:15.470 --> 1:02:18.150
<i>over 10 years ago at the Institute,</i>

1:02:18.150 --> 1:02:20.910
<i>but even back then, oversight authorities</i>

1:02:20.910 --> 1:02:23.730
<i>had already mandated filings 
to conduct an experiment.</i>

1:02:25.070 --> 1:02:28.180
<i>Optional Shifting poses 
a variety of problems.</i>

1:02:28.570 --> 1:02:32.170
<i>One of which is the possibility 
of it being used for crime.</i>

1:02:32.700 --> 1:02:34.180
<i>This goes without saying,</i>

1:02:34.180 --> 1:02:37.080
but your family's safety is the top priority.

1:02:37.750 --> 1:02:40.070
Still, depending on the outcome,

1:02:40.070 --> 1:02:43.100
the impact on the academic world 
and society will be large.

1:02:43.100 --> 1:02:44.820
We must proceed carefully.

1:02:45.710 --> 1:02:50.700
<i>What concerned us in this case was 
a different, more complicated problem.</i>

1:02:53.260 --> 1:02:54.260
I'm home.

1:03:05.180 --> 1:03:07.340
Kazune, it's me.

1:03:09.300 --> 1:03:10.960
Ryou, I'm coming in.

1:04:13.870 --> 1:04:15.750
You aren't cold out here?

1:04:15.750 --> 1:04:16.790
Papa!

1:04:17.660 --> 1:04:19.410
I bought these on the way.

1:04:19.410 --> 1:04:21.560
I want the cocoa!

1:04:26.450 --> 1:04:30.920
That fact that you're here means 
you knew what the picture was.

1:04:30.920 --> 1:04:33.620
No, I figured it out this morning.

1:04:34.050 --> 1:04:35.590
I didn't know before, really.

1:04:37.090 --> 1:04:38.100
Oh.

1:04:39.460 --> 1:04:43.810
At that point we were still 
in the same world.

1:04:45.670 --> 1:04:47.140
Should I open your cocoa?

1:04:47.140 --> 1:04:48.690
I'll have it later.

1:04:48.690 --> 1:04:49.830
Okay.

1:04:51.500 --> 1:04:53.190
I'll put it here, then.

1:04:53.190 --> 1:04:54.200
Okay.

1:04:54.830 --> 1:04:56.700
I brought this for Mama.

1:04:57.510 --> 1:05:00.780
Taking this off this doesn't mean your 
Optional Shift won't be noticed.

1:05:01.200 --> 1:05:03.480
It wasn't a logical choice.

1:05:03.480 --> 1:05:05.570
I hated that it wouldn't show 0.

1:05:06.060 --> 1:05:08.240
You're risking an identity diffusion.

1:05:21.700 --> 1:05:23.440
<i>"Identity diffusion."</i>

1:05:24.030 --> 1:05:29.150
<i>If a split occurs while you're
 Shifting to a far-off world,</i>

1:05:29.150 --> 1:05:31.650
<i>you get even farther 
from your original world.</i>

1:05:32.410 --> 1:05:35.780
<i>Also, from the perspective of 
the consciousness you swapped with,</i>

1:05:35.780 --> 1:05:38.460
<i>there ends up being two points of return,</i>

1:05:38.460 --> 1:05:42.000
<i>and every action causes
 complicated splits.</i>

1:05:43.210 --> 1:05:45.000
Let's go straight to the Institute and—

1:05:45.000 --> 1:05:45.870
No!

1:05:48.350 --> 1:05:50.420
I'm never leaving my son again!

1:05:52.730 --> 1:05:54.880
You aren't the Kazune of this world.

1:05:54.880 --> 1:05:56.550
I finally found him!

1:05:56.550 --> 1:05:57.390
No.

1:05:58.180 --> 1:05:59.390
He isn't "your" Ryou.

1:06:00.770 --> 1:06:01.890
Your...

1:06:02.730 --> 1:06:04.520
Your world's Ryou is...

1:06:08.820 --> 1:06:10.430
Where's Mama?

1:06:11.580 --> 1:06:14.150
Hey, tell me. Where'd Mama go?

1:06:17.160 --> 1:06:18.580
I have to face it.

1:06:19.230 --> 1:06:22.470
You aren't my Ryou anymore, are you?

1:06:23.270 --> 1:06:27.230
Only the one who died is my Ryou.

1:06:28.660 --> 1:06:29.940
My legs...

1:06:31.220 --> 1:06:32.940
wouldn't move!

1:06:34.360 --> 1:06:35.680
I was too scared...

1:06:37.080 --> 1:06:40.680
I couldn't save him!

1:06:42.180 --> 1:06:44.160
I'm sorry, Ryou.

1:06:44.790 --> 1:06:46.950
I couldn't protect you...

1:06:47.980 --> 1:06:49.420
I'm so sorry!

1:06:53.450 --> 1:06:56.650
Ryou, pick up your crayons.

1:06:56.650 --> 1:06:58.660
Okay. But...

1:06:59.930 --> 1:07:02.160
Make sure not to step on them, okay?

1:07:02.160 --> 1:07:03.220
Okay.

1:07:15.030 --> 1:07:16.490
It's not your fault.

1:07:16.490 --> 1:07:18.220
It <i>is</i> my fault!

1:07:18.810 --> 1:07:21.340
I killed him!

1:07:21.340 --> 1:07:24.220
And Koyomi must think that, too!

1:07:25.020 --> 1:07:27.840
He doesn't think that. I promise you.

1:07:27.840 --> 1:07:29.900
You don't get it!

1:07:29.900 --> 1:07:32.730
You got to go down the happy path!

1:07:32.730 --> 1:07:34.230
How could you possibly...

1:07:36.980 --> 1:07:38.520
Maybe I don't.

1:07:39.340 --> 1:07:40.900
But, I can tell.

1:07:42.100 --> 1:07:45.370
Because no matter what path, I'm still me.

1:07:47.440 --> 1:07:52.300
Right now, I'm waiting, hoping 
for Kazune to come back to me.

1:07:53.290 --> 1:07:58.880
So I'm sure that the me in your world 
is waiting for you to return, too.

1:08:07.270 --> 1:08:08.770
Look!

1:08:12.680 --> 1:08:13.930
They turned on!

1:08:13.930 --> 1:08:14.770
Did you see?

1:08:14.770 --> 1:08:16.740
The lights turned on!

1:08:18.940 --> 1:08:20.270
It's pretty, isn't it?

1:08:43.470 --> 1:08:45.680
Mr. Hidaka, what's that?

1:08:45.680 --> 1:08:50.640
Ah, yes. We're making a clone 
of the IP device for investigation.

1:08:57.790 --> 1:08:59.350
I finished it!

1:09:01.680 --> 1:09:04.350
Hey! Come back. You can't come in here.

1:09:04.350 --> 1:09:05.490
It's all right.

1:09:06.500 --> 1:09:08.160
It's not a problem.

1:09:08.860 --> 1:09:10.240
This is for you.

1:09:14.300 --> 1:09:16.070
This is what I wanted to see.

1:09:18.260 --> 1:09:19.580
Thank you.

1:09:26.180 --> 1:09:27.440
Kazune.

1:09:33.690 --> 1:09:36.440
Well then... Take care of yourself.

1:09:37.420 --> 1:09:39.440
You could really use a sense of tact.

1:09:40.900 --> 1:09:41.940
Sorry.

1:09:48.640 --> 1:09:51.270
<i>"It's not fair." That's what 
it looked like she said.</i>

1:09:53.310 --> 1:09:55.560
Why not us, too?

1:10:02.780 --> 1:10:05.390
Starting Optional Shift protocol.

1:10:05.390 --> 1:10:06.930
Vitals normal.

1:10:06.930 --> 1:10:09.230
Magnetic field set up.

1:10:09.230 --> 1:10:11.050
Beginning Optional Shift.

1:10:16.890 --> 1:10:19.310
Optional Shift complete.

1:10:20.110 --> 1:10:23.940
Vitals look good, but 
brain waves a little weak.

1:10:37.970 --> 1:10:42.440
<i>At the time, an IP Capsule could 
only be operated from the outside.</i>

1:10:43.150 --> 1:10:47.360
<i>I'm reasonably certain that 
Kazune 13's collaborator was me 13.</i>

1:10:48.030 --> 1:10:51.200
<i>They were probably driven from 
academia over this incident.</i>

1:10:52.330 --> 1:10:54.800
<i>Having lost both Ryou and their research,</i>

1:10:54.800 --> 1:10:57.490
<i>they would have to find another way to live.</i>

1:11:00.110 --> 1:11:04.900
<i>Because of the identity diffusion, 
this person was no longer 0 numerically.</i>

1:11:05.500 --> 1:11:08.300
<i>Her world had split, 
so when she Shifted back,</i>

1:11:08.300 --> 1:11:11.740
<i>she split into two people and 
ceased to be 0 numerically.</i>

1:11:13.530 --> 1:11:16.220
<i>But there was no one closer to 0 than her.</i>

1:11:17.410 --> 1:11:21.050
<i>From her frame of reference, 
Ryou and I weren't 0.</i>

1:11:26.860 --> 1:11:31.390
I once had someone talk to me about 
the significance of possibilities,

1:11:31.390 --> 1:11:34.390
but as an elementary 
schooler, I didn't get it.

1:11:34.390 --> 1:11:35.550
Okay.

1:11:36.130 --> 1:11:37.980
He said it was my homework.

1:11:38.900 --> 1:11:42.950
Except it seemed like he didn't 
have a clear answer himself.

1:11:42.950 --> 1:11:44.340
Wait, really?

1:11:44.940 --> 1:11:47.360
I still haven't found my answer.

1:11:47.360 --> 1:11:50.640
But, I do know I love possibilities.

1:11:51.770 --> 1:11:54.710
In trying to find the answer, 
I ended up loving them.

1:11:56.270 --> 1:11:57.660
This is about love?

1:11:57.660 --> 1:12:01.670
Regarding people, I want my love to 
also encompass their possibilities.

1:12:01.670 --> 1:12:04.670
Love that encompasses... 
their possibilities?

1:12:05.340 --> 1:12:09.170
Every part of them, in their entirety,

1:12:09.870 --> 1:12:12.590
including possibilities for
 both happiness and sadness.

1:12:14.950 --> 1:12:19.200
There must be as many possibilities 
for sadness as for happiness...

1:12:19.970 --> 1:12:24.360
As I see it, as long as we're alive, 
we'll go from one to the other in turn.

1:12:26.570 --> 1:12:31.640
For example, let's say there were 100 
worlds with 100 of me and 100 of you.

1:12:32.330 --> 1:12:36.280
I'm sure the 100 me's 
would love the 100 you's.

1:12:37.340 --> 1:12:40.850
Whether life was happy or sad.

1:12:40.850 --> 1:12:45.550
In each individual world, each 
individual me loves Kazune.

1:12:46.940 --> 1:12:48.840
Kazune, I—

1:12:59.710 --> 1:13:03.310
<i>I love every you, too.</i>

1:13:04.230 --> 1:13:08.830
<i>I love the you that saved me from 
the wild dog, and the you that didn't.</i>

1:13:08.830 --> 1:13:10.880
<i>Huh? What are you talking about?</i>

1:13:11.740 --> 1:13:13.080
<i>Oh, nothing.</i>

1:13:25.710 --> 1:13:29.270
<i>After that, we peacefully grew old,</i>

1:13:29.630 --> 1:13:35.160
<i>and went from being called "Mom" 
and "Dad" to "Grandpa" and "Grandma."</i>

1:13:35.930 --> 1:13:41.140
<i>Out of the people close to us, the older 
ones departed one after the other,</i>

1:13:41.140 --> 1:13:46.150
<i>but the front entryway of my home never 
opened for anything but the New Year.</i>

1:13:47.180 --> 1:13:49.570
<i>However, apparently it will soon.</i>

1:13:49.570 --> 1:13:52.440
<i>And the one who leaves 
through it will be me.</i>

1:13:53.130 --> 1:13:56.750
<i>It was just before my 
granddaughter Ai turned ten</i>

1:13:56.750 --> 1:13:59.160
<i>that the doctors broke the news to us.</i>

1:14:01.320 --> 1:14:04.100
<i>Kazune didn't say anything.</i>

1:14:06.600 --> 1:14:10.020
<i>Strangely, I felt calm.</i>

1:14:14.010 --> 1:14:15.680
Well, good night, dear.

1:14:17.350 --> 1:14:18.550
Good night.

1:14:21.450 --> 1:14:24.740
<i>I didn't want to burden my family,</i>

1:14:25.190 --> 1:14:27.990
<i>but partly at Ryou and Eri-san's suggestion,</i>

1:14:27.990 --> 1:14:33.330
<i>I decided to spend my final days in 
the familiar surroundings of my home.</i>

1:14:49.210 --> 1:15:00.220
<b></b>Kazune Takasaki-sama

1:15:05.070 --> 1:15:07.980
<i>"Kazune Takasaki-sama. Greetings.</i>

1:15:08.500 --> 1:15:10.670
<i>My name is Kazune Takigawa.</i>

1:15:11.500 --> 1:15:13.520
<i>I must start with an apology.</i>

1:15:14.020 --> 1:15:17.000
<i>I am sorry for Optional Shifting like this.</i>

1:15:17.620 --> 1:15:21.550
<i>I'm currently borrowing your 
hands to write this letter.</i>

1:15:23.380 --> 1:15:27.900
<i>The truth is, there is something 
I badly wish to ask of you.</i>

1:15:28.430 --> 1:15:30.000
<i>In order to do so,</i>

1:15:30.000 --> 1:15:33.500
<i>I need to tell you my story.</i>

1:15:33.500 --> 1:15:37.900
<i>The story of me and my world's 
Koyomi, Koyomi Hidaka.</i>

1:15:38.900 --> 1:15:43.360
<i>This might be end up being a long letter, 
but please bear with me to the end."</i>

1:15:48.510 --> 1:15:52.460
<i>Koyomi Hidaka and I are not married.</i>

1:15:48.850 --> 1:15:52.770
<b>Imaginary Science Research Institute</b>

1:15:53.260 --> 1:15:58.100
<i>We spent many years together, 
always as research partners.</i>

1:15:56.690 --> 1:15:59.490
<b>Entrance Ceremony</b>

1:15:58.990 --> 1:16:02.500
<i>The point of divergence 
between your world and ours</i>

1:16:02.500 --> 1:16:08.520
<i>appears to be which of his divorced parents
he chose to live with when he was seven.</i>

1:16:10.140 --> 1:16:14.480
<i>It was when I was in high school 
that I met Koyomi Hidaka.</i>

1:16:14.460 --> 1:16:16.460
<b>Entrance 
Ceremony</b>

1:16:15.160 --> 1:16:18.030
<i>He talked to no one and 
was frequently absent,</i>

1:16:18.030 --> 1:16:20.470
<i>yet had the highest grades. 
It got on my nerves.</i>

1:16:21.130 --> 1:16:24.280
<i>I unilaterally viewed him as my rival.</i>

1:16:25.510 --> 1:16:29.290
<i>So when I learned he 
wouldn't be attending college,</i>

1:16:29.290 --> 1:16:32.430
<i>I felt somehow relieved, 
and somehow lonely...</i>

1:16:32.430 --> 1:16:34.720
<i>a complicated bundle of feelings.</i>

1:16:35.760 --> 1:16:40.950
<i>Looking back on it now, my attitude 
might have been more than simple rivalry.</i>

1:16:37.570 --> 1:16:42.030
<b><b>First Semester Exams</b></b>

1:16:37.570 --> 1:16:42.030
<b><b>Hidaka
Koyomi</b></b>

1:16:37.570 --> 1:16:42.030
<b><b>Takigawa
 Kazune</b></b>

1:16:42.030 --> 1:16:43.590
<i>Whatever the case,</i>

1:16:43.590 --> 1:16:48.740
<i>I eventually joined the Research Institute, 
where our paths crossed again.</i>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>News</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>Latest Technology</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>Research Studies</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>Opinion</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>Data Science</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b>The Inevitable Event Radius</b>

1:16:47.830 --> 1:16:49.690
<b></b>(Imaginary Science Research Institute)

1:16:49.340 --> 1:16:52.100
<i>I suppose that's a misleading way to put it.</i>

1:16:52.670 --> 1:16:57.660
<i>I joined the Research Institute 
specifically to see him.</i>

1:17:13.910 --> 1:17:20.370
<i>Before long, he offered me an opportunity 
to join him in his secret research.</i>

1:17:21.010 --> 1:17:24.230
<i>Research into time travel.</i>

1:17:25.060 --> 1:17:26.720
<i>I was taken aback.</i>

1:17:26.720 --> 1:17:30.480
<i>It all seemed so ridiculous 
and lacking in common sense.</i>

1:17:31.640 --> 1:17:33.410
<i>I imagine you understand why.</i>

1:17:34.710 --> 1:17:36.670
<i>At first, I thought it was a joke.</i>

1:17:37.210 --> 1:17:39.010
<i>The best attempt at a joke</i>

1:17:39.010 --> 1:17:41.690
<i>that the Institute's biggest 
eccentric could muster.</i>

1:17:42.310 --> 1:17:45.560
<i>But, he wasn't the type of
 person who could tell jokes.</i>

1:17:46.260 --> 1:17:50.500
<i>I was witnessing him at his 
most heartfelt and earnest.</i>

1:17:51.570 --> 1:17:54.080
<i>As for why, it was because time travel</i>

1:17:54.080 --> 1:17:58.240
<i>was the sole way to save 
the ghost of the intersection.</i>

1:17:59.500 --> 1:18:04.070
<i>This ghost was his young sweetheart.</i>

1:19:07.930 --> 1:19:10.430
<b>Beds           Bedding</b>

1:19:07.930 --> 1:19:10.430
<b>Adjustable Beds</b>

1:19:53.500 --> 1:19:57.890
<i>A certain accident untethered 
her imaginality from her body,</i>

1:19:57.890 --> 1:20:00.420
<i>and she became a ghost at the intersection.</i>

1:20:01.620 --> 1:20:02.950
<i>"It's my fault,"</i>

1:20:03.520 --> 1:20:05.310
<i>he told me.</i>

1:20:06.040 --> 1:20:08.730
<i>And that thought alone</i>

1:20:08.730 --> 1:20:12.700
<i>drove him to continue his research 
to save her from that intersection.</i>

1:20:14.050 --> 1:20:16.160
<i>Looking at him, he seemed...</i>

1:20:16.160 --> 1:20:20.160
<i>I don't know, but somehow 
tinged with madness.</i>

1:20:21.360 --> 1:20:26.150
<i>But in another light, he looked just 
so incredibly pure and pitiful...</i>

1:20:27.180 --> 1:20:32.080
<i>It felt just like looking 
at a small, crying boy.</i>

1:20:33.630 --> 1:20:36.620
<i>And the answer this man had arrived at</i>

1:20:36.620 --> 1:20:38.020
<i>was time travel.</i>

1:20:39.680 --> 1:20:43.600
<i>Of course, not physical time travel,</i>

1:20:43.600 --> 1:20:46.150
<i>but time travel in the imaginary world.</i>

1:20:46.830 --> 1:20:50.050
<i>That is to say, Time Shifting.</i>

1:20:51.890 --> 1:20:53.600
Time Shifting...

1:20:55.970 --> 1:20:57.700
Just you wait!

1:20:57.700 --> 1:21:01.000
I swear I'll save you!

1:21:04.490 --> 1:21:06.680
You've done enough for me, Koyomi-kun.

1:21:08.740 --> 1:21:11.100
A whole lot of time has 
passed now, hasn't it?

1:21:12.690 --> 1:21:13.760
Shiori?

1:21:13.760 --> 1:21:15.510
Only for me...

1:21:16.970 --> 1:21:20.010
For you to be living 
your life solely for me...

1:21:20.770 --> 1:21:22.310
That isn't what I want.

1:21:23.340 --> 1:21:24.910
What are you saying?

1:21:24.910 --> 1:21:26.270
I promised, remember?

1:21:27.170 --> 1:21:29.230
I'd help you no matter what.

1:21:39.390 --> 1:21:44.960
<i>Koyomi and I spent decades 
investigating Time Shifting,</i>

1:21:44.960 --> 1:21:48.310
<i>and at last we discovered a way to do it.</i>

1:21:49.000 --> 1:21:54.300
<i>We also discovered Koyomi would be 
left brain dead as a result.</i>

1:21:55.000 --> 1:21:58.550
<i>I swear, he can be so 
self-centered, so self-righteous,</i>

1:21:58.550 --> 1:22:00.470
<i>so inconsiderate of his impact on others...</i>

1:22:01.320 --> 1:22:05.530
<i>Well, I suppose I'm the only one 
who will suffer an impact, but still...</i>

1:22:06.350 --> 1:22:11.350
<i>Regardless, his determination didn't waver, 
no matter the cost to him.</i>

1:22:12.670 --> 1:22:15.770
<i>I wasn't taken aback that time.</i>

1:22:16.740 --> 1:22:18.810
<i>As I could have predicted, it was just...</i>

1:22:18.810 --> 1:22:22.700
<i>a jerk giving no thought 
to me being left behind.</i>

1:22:22.700 --> 1:22:25.380
<i>In that case, why </i>not<i> follow 
through to the end, right?</i>

1:22:26.060 --> 1:22:28.940
<i>It was that kind of stubbornness,</i>

1:22:28.940 --> 1:22:31.880
<i>and the knowledge that I was 
the only one who </i>could<i>....</i>

1:22:33.470 --> 1:22:37.630
<i>We carefully selected 
a destination for the Time Shift.</i>

1:22:38.550 --> 1:22:42.130
<i>That destination was this world, your world.</i>

1:22:43.400 --> 1:22:48.170
<i>Koyomi wouldn't say a word about
 what happened between them.</i>

1:22:49.300 --> 1:22:53.140
<i>He only repeated that 
the accident wouldn't happen</i>

1:22:53.140 --> 1:22:56.040
<i>in a world where they never met.</i>

1:23:21.160 --> 1:23:24.680
I'm sorry you had to wait so long.

1:23:26.420 --> 1:23:27.650
What do you mean?

1:23:29.520 --> 1:23:34.420
We met as kids and you 
suffered your accident...

1:23:35.610 --> 1:23:38.650
It's been six decades since then.

1:23:39.540 --> 1:23:42.050
It's truly been a long time.

1:23:42.050 --> 1:23:43.380
Koyomi-kun?

1:23:44.520 --> 1:23:45.580
Shiori,

1:23:46.150 --> 1:23:47.380
this is farewell.

1:23:52.130 --> 1:23:56.290
<i>Yesterday, Koyomi executed the Time Shift.</i>

1:23:57.150 --> 1:23:59.180
<i>In our world,</i>

1:23:59.180 --> 1:24:02.210
<i>from the outside, it looks like
 an old man collapsed mid-research</i>

1:24:02.210 --> 1:24:05.160
<i>and fell into a state of brain death.</i>

1:24:06.430 --> 1:24:09.540
<i>But </i>I<i> know the truth.</i>

1:24:10.570 --> 1:24:13.660
<i>That he went to your word's past.</i>

1:24:15.050 --> 1:24:20.450
<i>Inside Koyomi Takasaki, 
the husband now sleeping at your side,</i>

1:24:21.190 --> 1:24:25.980
<i>dwells Koyomi Hidaka's imaginality, 
which joined with him at age seven.</i>

1:24:26.450 --> 1:24:29.370
<i>And within her counterpart in your world</i>

1:24:29.730 --> 1:24:33.370
<i>dwells <i>her</i> imaginality, which Koyomi saved.</i>

1:24:34.550 --> 1:24:37.700
<i>If we just let them live 
their respective lives,</i>

1:24:37.700 --> 1:24:39.980
<i>the two of them probably won't meet.</i>

1:24:40.950 --> 1:24:42.520
<i>That would be all right.</i>

1:24:43.220 --> 1:24:47.940
<i>After all, they chose a world where 
they would absolutely never meet.</i>

1:24:49.650 --> 1:24:53.060
<i>But, just before the Time Shift,</i>

1:24:53.060 --> 1:24:56.900
<i>Koyomi told me he made 
a promise with her to reunite.</i>

1:24:58.770 --> 1:25:01.860
<i>The promise was to meet at the intersection</i>

1:25:01.860 --> 1:25:04.030
<i>at the end of their lives in your world.</i>

1:25:05.330 --> 1:25:07.430
<i>According to theoretical predictions,</i>

1:25:07.430 --> 1:25:12.050
<i>the possibility of memories from my 
world remaining after the Time Shift</i>

1:25:12.050 --> 1:25:14.280
<i>can be considered essentially nonexistent.</i>

1:25:15.490 --> 1:25:20.620
<i>And so, it sounds like it was a way 
to persuade his young sweetheart.</i>

1:25:21.930 --> 1:25:24.460
<i>A promise that would be difficult to keep.</i>

1:25:25.940 --> 1:25:27.940
<i>Rather than a promise,</i>

1:25:27.940 --> 1:25:30.710
<i>you might say it was 
a faint hope on Koyomi's part,</i>

1:25:30.710 --> 1:25:32.860
<i>that such a miracle might occur.</i>

1:25:48.740 --> 1:25:53.850
<i>My desire at present is to 
bring about that miracle.</i>

1:25:55.180 --> 1:26:00.980
<i>It's true that deep down, a part of me 
didn't want the two of them to meet.</i>

1:26:02.290 --> 1:26:03.400
<i>But,</i>

1:26:05.680 --> 1:26:11.100
<i>I just input an entry about 
the Showa-dori intersection</i>

1:26:11.100 --> 1:26:12.600
<i>on your husband's IP device.</i>

1:26:15.060 --> 1:26:19.320
<b>Showa-dori Intersection</b>

1:26:19.330 --> 1:26:24.840
<i>I ask that in one month's time, you please 
send him off to the site of the promise.</i>

1:26:27.790 --> 1:26:29.590
<i>I'll be honest with you.</i>

1:26:30.810 --> 1:26:36.380
<i>I imagine that as another me, you 
already have in inkling of this, but...</i>

1:26:37.030 --> 1:26:39.340
<i>I love the Koyomi of my world.</i>

1:26:39.340 --> 1:26:41.660
<i>I love Koyomi Hidaka.</i>

1:26:43.730 --> 1:26:47.740
<i>Of course, that love was not 
part of a romantic relationship,</i>

1:26:47.740 --> 1:26:51.320
<i>nor was it a love that led to marriage.</i>

1:26:52.500 --> 1:26:59.060
<i>But all the more, I can feel that 
I spend my life loving Koyomi Hidaka.</i>

1:26:59.060 --> 1:27:01.860
<i>At this moment, I feel it, deeply.</i>

1:27:04.040 --> 1:27:07.060
<i>I know this is a self-centered request.</i>

1:27:07.740 --> 1:27:12.370
<i>After all, for you—though for me, as well—</i>

1:27:13.250 --> 1:27:17.680
<i>this would mean sending the person 
you love to someone else's side.</i>

1:27:19.370 --> 1:27:23.520
<i>I ask that you let my love 
act through your feelings.</i>

1:27:28.830 --> 1:27:31.580
<i>Happiness and sadness in life come to an end.</i>

1:27:33.020 --> 1:27:35.890
<i>But one person caring for another—</i>

1:27:36.920 --> 1:27:40.900
<i>if I can believe that feeling continues on,</i>

1:27:45.940 --> 1:27:48.560
<i>the fact that we're born 
into a certain world,</i>

1:27:50.780 --> 1:27:51.910
<i>meet,</i>

1:27:52.950 --> 1:27:54.550
<i>live,</i>

1:27:54.550 --> 1:27:57.910
<i>and die will have meaning.</i>

1:27:57.910 --> 1:28:00.780
<i>I'll be able to say that 
at the end of my life.</i>

1:28:13.480 --> 1:28:14.930
Morning.

1:28:14.930 --> 1:28:16.610
<i>P.S.</i>

1:28:16.610 --> 1:28:20.460
<i>The truth is, I Shifted here once before.</i>

1:28:20.460 --> 1:28:26.320
<i>I saw your aquamarine ring then, and 
I copied you by getting one of my own.</i>

1:28:26.320 --> 1:28:27.650
<i>Sorry.</i>

1:28:50.580 --> 1:28:51.880
Error?

1:28:51.880 --> 1:29:00.520
<b>Please wait until the traffic signal changes.</b>

1:28:51.880 --> 1:29:00.520
<b>Please wait until the traffic signal changes.</b>

1:28:52.940 --> 1:28:54.570
Where am I?

1:28:55.420 --> 1:28:57.080
How far away did I end up?

1:29:03.560 --> 1:29:06.550
<i>Assuming a Parallel Shift occurred,</i>

1:29:06.970 --> 1:29:09.790
<i>I'm not in that world where 
the girl in the crosswalk is,</i>

1:29:09.790 --> 1:29:12.720
<i>so there's nothing I can do about that.</i>

1:29:14.020 --> 1:29:19.000
<i>I can only pray that the me 
I swapped with doesn't get confused...</i>

1:29:21.190 --> 1:29:22.830
Prayer, huh?

1:29:25.200 --> 1:29:27.910
<i>But, why does it say there's an error?</i>

1:29:27.910 --> 1:29:31.710
<i>Assuming inability to measure, 
one possibility is...</i>

1:29:32.090 --> 1:29:36.030
<i>If by chance, my imaginality itself changed—</i>

1:29:36.030 --> 1:29:37.290
<i>Hold on.</i>

1:29:37.290 --> 1:29:40.110
<i>This idea sounds like 
something I've heard before.</i>

1:29:40.110 --> 1:29:41.620
<i>What was the paper?</i>

1:29:42.370 --> 1:29:45.240
<i>Did I hear it at a conference?</i>

1:29:45.240 --> 1:29:47.570
<i>Who did I discuss it with?</i>

1:30:23.070 --> 1:30:27.080
Excuse me. Are you alright?

1:30:27.080 --> 1:30:28.500
Hello? Sir?

1:30:28.500 --> 1:30:29.710
My medicine.

1:30:30.490 --> 1:30:32.940
Get... my medicine.

1:30:37.780 --> 1:30:39.180
Is this it?

1:30:39.900 --> 1:30:42.190
Oh, I'll open it for you.

1:30:44.460 --> 1:30:46.580
Here. Can you take it?

1:31:11.070 --> 1:31:13.270
I'm all right now.

1:31:13.860 --> 1:31:15.440
You were a great help.

1:31:16.950 --> 1:31:18.560
Thank goodness.

1:31:20.300 --> 1:31:21.560
Thank you.

1:31:23.640 --> 1:31:28.370
Excuse me, ma'am. If possible, 
might I ask your name?

1:31:28.900 --> 1:31:31.960
My name's not important. I'm nobody special.

1:31:32.370 --> 1:31:33.460
Huh?

1:31:34.570 --> 1:31:37.470
Oh, you see,

1:31:37.930 --> 1:31:41.180
it was something on my bucket list.

1:31:41.910 --> 1:31:44.970
To say, "My name's not important. 
I'm nobody special."

1:31:45.590 --> 1:31:47.930
Well, my goodness.

1:31:47.930 --> 1:31:50.070
I'm honored I could help.

1:31:50.070 --> 1:31:51.000
Your hat.

1:31:51.000 --> 1:31:52.040
Sorry for the trouble.

1:31:52.890 --> 1:31:54.370
Woops.

1:31:54.370 --> 1:31:55.980
Oh my, this...

1:31:56.450 --> 1:31:59.160
Yes, it's a ladies' hat.

1:31:59.820 --> 1:32:01.630
I'm borrowing it.

1:32:01.630 --> 1:32:03.160
I see.

1:32:05.050 --> 1:32:09.590
Um, haven't we met somewhere before, ma'am?

1:32:09.590 --> 1:32:10.750
Have we?

1:32:13.780 --> 1:32:16.570
Pardon me, but could I get your name?

1:32:16.570 --> 1:32:17.860
It's Takasaki.

1:32:24.610 --> 1:32:28.770
I'm sorry. I just don't seem 
to recognize you or your name.

1:32:30.770 --> 1:32:32.060
Is that so?

1:32:33.170 --> 1:32:38.390
Then perhaps we might have 
met in a parallel world.

1:32:38.390 --> 1:32:40.140
Oh, that's true.

1:32:40.140 --> 1:32:42.480
That is possible, isn't it?

1:32:45.700 --> 1:32:46.900
Um...

1:32:48.390 --> 1:32:52.430
Is your life... happy?

1:32:55.480 --> 1:32:56.720
Yes.

1:32:56.720 --> 1:32:59.780
It <i>is</i> happy. Very much so.

1:33:02.500 --> 1:33:04.500
I'm glad.

1:33:04.500 --> 1:33:05.550
Well, then.

1:33:07.900 --> 1:33:09.440
Take care.

1:33:09.960 --> 1:33:11.440
Goodbye.

1:33:12.460 --> 1:33:13.690
Goodbye.

1:33:14.600 --> 1:33:15.940
Thank you.

1:33:41.740 --> 1:33:43.090
Welcome back.

1:33:43.740 --> 1:33:44.920
Thanks.

1:33:45.700 --> 1:33:47.700
You waited for me?

1:33:47.700 --> 1:33:48.920
Maybe.

1:33:50.150 --> 1:33:51.220
By the way,

1:33:52.430 --> 1:33:53.730
who are you?

1:33:53.730 --> 1:33:54.760
Huh?

1:33:56.070 --> 1:34:01.230
My IEPP is reading "error", so 
I don't know where I am right now.

1:34:01.770 --> 1:34:03.800
Don't phrase it like that.

1:34:03.800 --> 1:34:05.250
Sorry, sorry.

1:34:06.050 --> 1:34:08.570
Look. Think it's a malfunction?

1:34:10.430 --> 1:34:12.100
That's unusual.

1:34:12.610 --> 1:34:17.500
I left to go to the Showa-dori 
intersection. Is that accurate?

1:34:17.860 --> 1:34:20.810
Yes. At 10.

1:34:22.710 --> 1:34:25.120
Even if I did Parallel Shift,

1:34:25.120 --> 1:34:28.360
I seem to be in the neighborhood, 
so I guess it'll go back to 0 soon.

1:34:30.150 --> 1:34:33.490
So, this mysterious appointment. 
How did it go?

1:34:34.150 --> 1:34:37.110
Right. Nobody showed up.

1:34:37.690 --> 1:34:38.900
Oh.

1:34:42.410 --> 1:34:45.420
Maybe my schedule malfunctioned, too.

1:34:46.170 --> 1:34:50.010
But, I did have a lovely 
interaction with someone.

1:34:50.630 --> 1:34:53.940
Who was it? An old acquaintance?

1:34:53.940 --> 1:34:57.340
No. Someone I don't know.

1:34:57.340 --> 1:35:03.510
She said her life was happy, 
and I was very happy about that.

1:35:08.810 --> 1:35:11.010
Wasn't she a total stranger?

1:35:11.010 --> 1:35:16.020
Yeah. I took pleasure 
in a stranger's happiness.

1:35:16.720 --> 1:35:19.840
I realized that the reason I could 
take joy in a stranger's happiness

1:35:19.840 --> 1:35:23.290
is because I've known happiness myself.

1:35:24.650 --> 1:35:26.030
Nice and simple.

1:35:26.550 --> 1:35:27.670
It is.

1:35:28.310 --> 1:35:31.980
When I try to put it into simple 
terms, that's just how it goes...

1:35:33.950 --> 1:35:37.820
Kazune, because I had you with me,

1:35:37.820 --> 1:35:40.170
my life right now is happy.

1:35:44.140 --> 1:35:47.170
Tell that to your Kazune 0.

1:35:47.500 --> 1:35:48.910
I absolutely will.

1:35:49.980 --> 1:35:53.180
But I want to tell <i>every</i> you.

1:35:56.200 --> 1:36:01.350
<i>At the same time, to each me who 
loved someone who wasn't Kazune:</i>

1:36:01.930 --> 1:36:05.690
<i>Because you loved someone other than her,</i>

1:36:05.690 --> 1:36:08.890
<i>I got to love Kazune.</i>

1:36:09.580 --> 1:36:10.910
<i>Thank you.</i>

1:36:12.330 --> 1:36:14.460
Grandpa, welcome back!

1:36:15.020 --> 1:36:16.360
I'm home.

1:36:17.140 --> 1:36:21.080
I take it you watched Grandpa 
go out instead of studying?

1:36:22.100 --> 1:36:24.850
I couldn't help it. I was so curious!

1:36:26.080 --> 1:36:28.510
Ai sure has a strong sense of curiosity.

1:36:29.220 --> 1:36:31.190
She wanted to look out for you.

1:36:33.870 --> 1:36:39.700
<i>And finally, to the someone who wasn't 
Kazune that loved a different me:</i>

1:36:41.710 --> 1:36:45.420
<i>I pray that you and the person you love</i>

1:36:45.930 --> 1:36:50.120
<i>find happiness somewhere out in the world.</i>

1:36:57.120 --> 1:37:02.920
<b></b>To Every You I've Loved Before
